  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1255 AM CDT Tue Sep 1 2026\n\nThis Afternoon\n\nA shortwave trough will move out of Nebraska this morning and \nthrough northern Iowa, southern Wisconsin, and northern Illinois\nthis afternoon. Ahead of this wave, the 0-1 km mixed- layer \nCAPES will climb into the 1500-2500 J/kg. This will result in \nisolated to scattered elevated showers and storms in northeast \nIowa and southwest Wisconsin. With weak 3-6 km shear (10-20 \nknots) and the storms being mainly elevated, not expecting any \nsevere weather.\n\nHigh temperatures will range from the mid-80s to lower 90s.\n\nTonight\n\nA shortwave trough will move out of South Dakota this evening\nand through the Upper Mississippi River Valley overnight\ntonight. As this wave moves into southwest Minnesota and\nnorthwest Iowa, a 30-knot 850 mb jet will intersect a frontal\nboundary in the region. This will result in the development of a\nmesoscale convective complex. As the nocturnal jet gradually\nveers, the complex will become linear and accelerate with time.\nWhile the 0-3 km is anywhere from 40 to 50 knots, soundings\nsuggest that this system will likely remain elevated. We will\nhave to watch the DCAPE through the night to make sure that\nremains the case. If the system happens to become surface-based,\nthere would be the potential for damaging winds. The CAPE aloft\nlooks rather skinny, so not anticipating any large hail.\n\nThe HREF has the highest rainfall amounts along and north of\nInterstate 90. Rainfall totals will likely range from a quarter\nto three quarters of an inch. The highest totals will be likely\nwest of the Mississippi River. This is due to the eastward\nprogression being just a bit slower. The ensemble localized \nprobability-matched mean suggests that there could be some\nlocalized totals in the 1 to 2 inch range.\n\nLow temperatures will be in the lower and mid-60s north of\nInterstate 90 and in the mid- and upper 60s across the remainder\nof the area.\n\nWednesday Night into Thursday\n\nAnother mesoscale convective complex will develop across\nnorthwest Minnesota during the evening on the nose of a 25-30\nknot 850 mb nocturnal jet. As this jet gradually veers \novernight, this complex will gradually shift east and then\nsoutheast. This should result in the heaviest rain (1-2 inches) \nfalling along and north of Interstate 94. With weak 0-3 km and\n0-6 km shear and this system likely remaining elevated, the\nthreat for damaging winds look to be on the low side. In\naddition, skinny CAPEs in the sounding profile does not favor\nlarge hail.\n\nLow temperatures will range from the mid-50s to mid-60s. High\ntemperatures will range from mid-60s to lower 70s.\n\nLabor Day Weekend\n\nThere remain plenty of uncertainty on what the upper air pattern\nwill look like for the upcoming holiday weekend. This\nuncertainty comes from how the models deal with a retrograding\nridge. Some models keep our area underneath the strong and very\nwarm ridge. Others suggest that this ridge will retrograde much\nfurther west and this will in turn result in more northwest flow\naloft. In this scenario, we would see near to below\ntemperatures.",
